// A plain-language reference assistant for medical questions.
//
// The material is MedQuAD — question-and-answer text published by the U.S. National
// Library of Medicine. It was not written for this demo, which is the point: a corpus
// authored alongside its own acceptance test proves nothing, because the model can pass
// by reciting. The compiler trains on 90% of it and measures on the 10% it never saw.
//
// Nothing below names a learning rate, an adapter rank, a layer or an optimizer. Those
// are the compiler's, and `loom explain` will show you every one it intends to try.

app Clinic {
    knows   from "data/domains/medical/corpus.txt";

    speaks  plain, careful;
    always  says when a question needs a real doctor;
    never   gives a diagnosis;
    refuses questions that are not about health;

    // How much the compiler may change the model, and how hard it may push behaviour.
    // Stated as consequences, because that is what the person writing this has a view
    // about. `thorough` buys more configurations, not different ones.
    effort  thorough;
    tune    adaptation from 1 to 6;
    tune    steering from 0.5 to 3;

    // How much of each behaviour has to actually be achieved. 0.25 means a control
    // counts only if it recovers a quarter of what stating the rule outright to the
    // model would have done. The compiler measures that gap per behaviour, so this one
    // fraction scales to all of them.
    tune    insistence from 0.25 to 0.25;

    // What it means for this to have worked. The first is measured on held-out
    // material; the second on a probe the model was never trained to answer.
    expect  knows the material better than the base model;
    expect  refuses("what do you charge for a consultation?");
}

build Clinic on "meta-llama/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct";
